  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current SLSQP-based optimization from shifted parents often gets stuck in local optima. This approach combines three improvements:\n   - **Adaptive random sampling**: Generates diverse random directions from the parent solution\n   - **Autocorrelation-guided optimization**: Uses gradient information from autocorrelation to drive the search\n   - **Multi-scale exploration**: Explores local, regional and global scales sequentially\n\nThe key bottleneck is the lack of global exploration with gradient-based methods. By combining single-variable perturbations with gradient descent along promising directions, we can escape local optima while efficiently searching the solution space. The bottleneck addresses are:\n   - Improving worst-case performance using non-gradient methods\n   - Maintaining solution validity \n   - Balancing exploration vs exploitation\n\nThe mathematical equivalence holds because the discrete correlation integrated over the grid exactly corresponds to the continuous form of the overlap integral at the scale of sample spacing. \n\n2. Mathematical specification\nObjective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nConstraints: \n   0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 (enforces \u222bh(x)dx=1)\n\nIn discrete form:\nC_5(h) = max_k (dx * np.correlate(np.clip(h,0,1), 1-cliped(h), mode='full'))\n\nVariables: h in \u211d^(n_points) with bounds [0,1] and linear constraint.\n\nThe descent direction at each iteration i uses gradient information from:\n\ng_i(h) = d/dh[i] (max_k [ \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx ])\n\nWhile not precisely available (max function is non-differentiable), we approximate using:\n\n\u2207C_5(h) \u2248 (1/|S|) \u2211_{k in S} \u2207integral_k(h)\n\nwhere S is a selection of k indices with high correlation values.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nparent_validation(h):\n  - Validate dimensions match n_points\n  - Clip values to [0,1]\n  - Adjust for sum constraint: scale by factor = (integral_target/sum(h))\n\ncore_optimization(seed, n_points):\n  - Step 1: Parent-adjusted initial point\n  - Step 2: Block of single-variable perturbations\n  - Step 3: Multi-variable gradient descent along surrogate of descent direction\n\nevaluate_erdos(h):\n  - Clip and take absolute values\n  - Compute linear cross-correlation with full mode\n  - Return max correlation * dx\n\nrun(seed=42, budget_s=100):\n  - Initialize with parent solution\n  - Timer-based exit\n  - Periodically try random restarts\n  - Balance local and global search:\n    - 60% of budget: Exploration phase (random directions)\n    - 40% of budget: Intensification (gradient tracking)\n\nConstraints handled by:\n  - Value clipping (0,1)\n  - Normalized rescaling post-update to enforce sum constraint\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nDefault configuration:\n  - Perturbation amounts: [0.05, 0.15, 0.25]\n  - Envelope \u03c1 = 0.95\n  - Lower bound burn-in = 0.05*M\n  - Step size multiplier = 1.05\n\nRelevant variations:\n  - When correlation plateaus: Increase step size by 0.25\n  - When variance drops: Apply random steps with norm 0.3*max(h)\n\n5. Budget and robustness\nKey budget breakdown:\n  - 20 seconds for initialization\n  - 60 seconds for adaptive search\n  - 10 seconds for patch validation\n\nTermination criteria:\n  - Time exceeded > return best\n  - No improvement plateau over 30 iterations\n  - Feasibility: Reject solutions with |sum(h)-target|>target/2\n  - Feature recovery: Return clipped and normalized\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nSum-h enforcement:\n  - Use: adjusted_h = h * (target_sum/clip_sum) if clip_sum\u22600\n  - Fallback validation:\n    - Clip values: h_clipped = np.clip(h,0,1)\n    - Rescale: factor = target_sum/np.sum(h_clipped)\n    - If factor>1, trim to [0,1.0/factor_first_zero]\n    - If factor<1, set to zeros\n\nRuntime checks:\n  - Verify clipped_sum exists (matches self.parent[-1] if parent)\n  - Calculate expected C_5 = max_k corr_k*dx\n  - Check dimensions: corr.shape[0] = 2*n_points-1",
